>> title: Watercress and Honey Date Soup
>> category: Soups
>> tags: pork, watercress, honey dates, apricot kernels, cantonese, old-fire soup, autumn, stovetop
>> counters: The Soup Pot
>> aka: 西洋菜蜜棗豬骨湯, sai joeng coi mat zou zyu gwat tong, sai yeung choi mat jou jue gwat tong, watercress and honey date soup, watercress pork bone soup, 老火湯, lo fo tong
>> servings: 6
>> time: 3 hr 15 min
>> slack: narrow — the second handful of watercress has about ten minutes; left longer it goes the same drab olive as the first
>> step.1: 清潤肺燥 — the autumn pot. The watercress goes in twice: half at the start for the broth, half near the end for colour.
>> step.2: blanch from cold, then rinse the bones
>> step.3: rinse the dried goods
>> step.4: simmer 2 1/2 hr, barely a quiver
>> step.5: stir the second half of the watercress in, 10 min
>> step.6: season at the end, never at the start
>> step.7: The broth is the dish. The solids are spent.

The autumn pot — 清潤肺燥, the dry season — and the one made in a house where somebody has been coughing. The watercress goes in twice: half at the start to give the broth, half near the end for the colour.

Blanch @pork neck bones{2%lb}(900 g) from cold in @water{2%qt}(1.9 L) in a #soup pot{}, ~blanch{10%min} up to a rolling boil, then pour the whole lot away, rinse every bone under the tap and scrub the pot out.

Rinse @honey dates{3}(mat zou; sweetens a pot that has no sugar in it — the word used is 潤, moistening) and @apricot kernels{1/4%cup}(naam bak hang; the sweet and the bitter kind together, about three to one, and the pairing is the point — the bitter kind goes in small and always cooked through, never raw) under cold water, picking out any grit.

Simmer @&(~2)blanched bones{} with @&(~1)dried goods{}, @watercress{12%oz}(340 g; half the bunch, washed hard — it will cook down to almost nothing, and that is the point) and @cold water{3%qt}(2.8 L; the pot is filled once and not topped up) in the rinsed #soup pot{}, lid ajar, ~simmer{2 1/2%hr} at a bare quiver, unstirred.

Stir @watercress{12%oz}(340 g; the other half, in whole sprigs) into @&(~1)soup{} and ~simmer{10%min} more, which is where the green in the bowl comes from.

Season @&(~1)soup{} with @kosher salt{1%tsp}, at the end and never at the start.

The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.
